field/key "field-11" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-32399941-82c0-5d5a-a312-a1e1997e4e44" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d7220373-d119-5591-850e-24ed7375c3d9" "answer-e9f5d90b-26ca-53c0-9a57-4c841c19feec" "answer-32399941-82c0-5d5a-a312-a1e1997e4e44" "answer-70e9f5a4-a29f-52f3-bf78-f68e822a4cd2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7220373-d119-5591-850e-24ed7375c3d9" :answer/id #uuid "d7220373-d119-5591-850e-24ed7375c3d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9f5d90b-26ca-53c0-9a57-4c841c19feec" :answer/id #uuid "e9f5d90b-26ca-53c0-9a57-4c841c19feec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32399941-82c0-5d5a-a312-a1e1997e4e44" :answer/id #uuid "32399941-82c0-5d5a-a312-a1e1997e4e44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70e9f5a4-a29f-52f3-bf78-f68e822a4cd2" :answer/id #uuid "70e9f5a4-a29f-52f3-bf78-f68e822a4cd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163233 :tutorial/content "We can compute a singular value decomposition (SVD) of any matrix! The algorithm for computing an SVD of a rectangular $m\\times n$ matrix $(m\\neq n)$ is similar to the algorithm for square matrices.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/f6/f65e0eee905da85ce0be4bbf39546c2b31c2bac082ff00a6a1d54fd5b89a4a4e.svg)\n\nNotice that $A$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix. So, to compute the SVD of $A,$ we must write\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{blue}{3}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}}$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.**  Find the singular values of $A.$ Computing the eigenvalues of $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 4, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1} =  2,  \\qquad \\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} =  \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T A$). Seeking non-zero solutions of $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_1 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ and $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_2 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvectors \n![](images/07/07085f021218ff17060b3c1a9099e1cabad24b3d12269f97f1fe50d49b970f6b.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A.$ Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the first two columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $U$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix. However, we do not have the third singular value, so we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n![](images/71/71f89dbcee33bce284b3085b872fdb9da2cc41166fe8a0bd26ef0bb5fe6876aa.svg) \nsuch that \n![](images/9e/9edc744372650e86c27f31ef15ac575cc8203803bdc5fe5730390727fd37146a.svg)\nSolving this system, we get the following nonzero normalized solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c2/c2bbd771d886da6b3a369b7e03345d1be75c18c26c66590598581d2d99590524.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]}] "assertions" [["question-47b88835-9941-5b00-8cef-a75a2de591b3" :question/id #uuid "47b88835-9941-5b00-8cef-a75a2de591b3"] ["question-47b88835-9941-5b00-8cef-a75a2de591b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-363832"] ["question-47b88835-9941-5b00-8cef-a75a2de591b3" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | $\\frac{4}{5}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | ${x}_{1}$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $0$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"] ["question-47b88835-9941-5b00-8cef-a75a2de591b3" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{1}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"] ["question-47b88835-9941-5b00-8cef-a75a2de591b3" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-fa7da9c9-345c-5889-a4c3-151cdf9c9850" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa7da9c9-345c-5889-a4c3-151cdf9c9850"] ["answer-5fe66d81-a58f-5131-a76a-bdf41bd97bfd" :answer/id #uuid "5fe66d81-a58f-5131-a76a-bdf41bd97bfd"] ["answer-5fe66d81-a58f-5131-a76a-bdf41bd97bfd" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-5fe66d81-a58f-5131-a76a-bdf41bd97bfd" :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}"] ["answer-2f4acafd-ef69-5bad-9742-8f157db9faf9" :answer/id #uuid "2f4acafd-ef69-5bad-9742-8f157db9faf9"] ["answer-2f4acafd-ef69-5bad-9742-8f157db9faf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-2f4acafd-ef69-5bad-9742-8f157db9faf9" :answer/value "0"] ["answer-1da4de33-d31e-535e-8ef1-9058622f152f" :answer/id #uuid "1da4de33-d31e-535e-8ef1-9058622f152f"] ["answer-1da4de33-d31e-535e-8ef1-9058622f152f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-1da4de33-d31e-535e-8ef1-9058622f152f" :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}"] ["answer-a5db6f71-d5bb-531a-adbe-06783eaedc8e" :answer/id #uuid "a5db6f71-d5bb-531a-adbe-06783eaedc8e"] ["answer-a5db6f71-d5bb-531a-adbe-06783eaedc8e" :